# Flask Application Environment Variables
# 
# SECURITY WARNING: Never commit your actual .env file to version control!
# This is a template file. Copy it to .env and fill in your actual values.
#
# To generate secure secrets, you can use Python:
#   python -c "import secrets; print('SECRET_KEY=' + secrets.token_urlsafe(32))"
#   python -c "import secrets; print('SECURITY_PASSWORD_SALT=' + secrets.token_urlsafe(16))"
#
# Or use the generate_secrets.py script in the scripts/ directory.
#
# Best Practices:
# - Use different secrets for development, staging, and production
# - Rotate secrets periodically (especially if compromised)
# - Use a secrets management service in production (AWS Secrets Manager, etc.)
# - Never share secrets via email, chat, or unencrypted channels
#
# References:
# - OWASP Flask Security Cheat Sheet: https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Flask_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html
# - 12-Factor App: https://12factor.net/config
# - Flask Security Documentation: https://flask-security-too.readthedocs.io/

# Flask Secret Key
# Used for: session management, CSRF protection, signing cookies
# Generate with: secrets.token_urlsafe(32) (minimum 32 bytes recommended)
SECRET_KEY=your-secret-key-here-generate-a-secure-random-value

# Flask-Security Password Salt
# Used for: password hashing to prevent rainbow table attacks
# Generate with: secrets.token_urlsafe(16) (minimum 16 bytes recommended)
SECURITY_PASSWORD_SALT=your-password-salt-here-generate-a-secure-random-value
